---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import LegalLayout from "../components/LegalLayout.astro";

const title = "Privacy Policy · WeHale";
const description =
  "How WeHale collects, uses, and protects your data. Privacy policy for the WeHale app and site.";
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} solidHeader>
  <LegalLayout heading="Privacy Policy" lastUpdated="14 October 2026">
    <h2>1. Who we are</h2>
    <p>
      Andningsappen Varberg AB (trading as "WeHale", "we", "us", "our") is the
      controller of the personal data described in this policy.
    </p>
    <p>
      Andningsappen Varberg AB<br />
      Organisation number: 559496-1491<br />
      Boråsgatan 12 B, 432 45 Varberg, Sweden<br />
      Email: <a href="mailto:support@wehale.io">support@wehale.io</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      We have not appointed a data protection officer, because we are not
      required to. For anything in this policy, write to
      <a href="mailto:support@wehale.io">support@wehale.io</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      This policy covers the WeHale app for iPhone and Android, our servers, and
      the emails and notifications we send. Section 11 covers our website.
    </p>

    <h2>2. What we collect and why</h2>
    <p>
      We collect what the app needs to work. Here it is in plain words, with the
      reason and the legal basis under the EU General Data Protection Regulation
      (GDPR) for each.
    </p>

    <h3>Your account</h3>
    <p>
      Your email address and your name (you type them, or Apple or Google give
      them to us when you use Sign in with Apple or Sign in with Google). With
      email sign-up we also store a hashed password. We never see your Apple or
      Google password. We use this to run your account and sign you in. Legal
      basis: performing our contract with you (Art. 6(1)(b)).
    </p>

    <h3>Your practice</h3>
    <p>
      Which sessions you start and finish, how long you listen, the ratings you
      give, sessions you like, your weekly goal, your reminders (time and days),
      and your progress and milestones. We use this to show your progress and to
      unlock what you have access to. Legal basis: contract (Art. 6(1)(b)).
    </p>

    <h3>Your answers when you start</h3>
    <p>
      The goals and needs you choose, your experience level, how you heard about
      us, and your reminder time. We use them to suggest sessions. Legal basis:
      contract (Art. 6(1)(b)).
    </p>

    <h3>Check-ins</h3>
    <p>
      A rating from 1 to 5 before and after a session, and the mood you pick on
      Home (for example "Stressed" or "Tired"). These are your own subjective
      ratings. We use them to show how you feel over time and to recommend
      sessions, including with the AI service described in section 4. We do not
      use them to diagnose or treat anything, and WeHale is not a medical
      product. Legal basis: contract (Art. 6(1)(b)).
    </p>

    <h3>Your subscription</h3>
    <p>
      Whether you have a membership, which plan, when it started, renews or
      ends, and the purchase receipt reference. Apple and Google take the
      payment; we never see your card. RevenueCat, our subscription service,
      receives this to confirm what you may use. Legal basis: contract
      (Art. 6(1)(b)), and for accounting records a legal obligation
      (Art. 6(1)(c)).
    </p>

    <h3>Support and feedback</h3>
    <p>
      When you write to us in the app (Messages), send feedback, give a score,
      or attach a screenshot, we store what you send, with your account. We use
      it to help you and to improve the app. Legal basis: contract for support
      requests (Art. 6(1)(b)), and our legitimate interest in improving the app
      for feedback and scores (Art. 6(1)(f)).
    </p>

    <h3>Your device and the app's health</h3>
    <p>
      Device type, operating system, app version, and crash and performance
      reports. We use them to find and fix faults and to keep the service
      secure. Legal basis: legitimate interest (Art. 6(1)(f)).
    </p>

    <h3>Your connection</h3>
    <p>
      Like every server, ours receives your IP address when the app contacts it.
      We use it to protect the service against abuse, and our providers may
      derive an approximate country from it. Legal basis: legitimate interest
      (Art. 6(1)(f)).
    </p>

    <h3>Notifications</h3>
    <p>
      If you allow notifications, we store a push token for your device and your
      reminder settings, and we send the reminders you ask for and, from time to
      time, other messages about your practice or your offer (for example a
      reminder before an offer ends). Legal basis: consent, which you give in
      the system prompt and can withdraw in your device settings or in Profile
      &amp; Settings (Art. 6(1)(a)).
    </p>

    <h3>Partner and campaign links</h3>
    <p>
      If you arrive through a partner link or QR code, or type a code, we store
      the code, the link's channel and reference, and what it gave you, so that
      the right offer is shown and we can tell a partner how many people joined
      (a count, not your name). Legal basis: contract for the offer itself
      (Art. 6(1)(b)) and legitimate interest for the counting (Art. 6(1)(f)).
    </p>

    <p>
      We do not collect your location, contacts, camera or microphone. The photo
      library is opened only if you choose to attach a screenshot to feedback.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Usage analytics and install attribution</h2>

    <h3>Our own usage analytics</h3>
    <p>
      The app records events such as screens viewed, sessions started and
      finished, and purchase steps, tied to your account, and sends them to our
      server. We use them to see what works and to fix problems. You can turn
      this off at any time in Profile &amp; Settings under "Usage analytics".
      Turning it off stops the app sending these events and deletes the ones
      still waiting on your device. It is switched on by default. Legal basis:
      legitimate interest (Art. 6(1)(f)).
    </p>

    <h3>Install attribution (AppsFlyer)</h3>
    <p>
      The app includes AppsFlyer's measurement software. When you first open the
      app it tells AppsFlyer that the app was installed and opened, and which
      link or ad campaign led to it, so we can see which channels bring people
      to WeHale. After you sign in, it connects those events to your WeHale user
      ID. A selection of in-app events (sign-up, onboarding finished, sessions
      started and finished, session titles and categories, checkout steps and
      the price shown, trial and subscription events) is forwarded to AppsFlyer
      so a campaign can be measured against what people do. Forwarding of
      in-app events follows your "Usage analytics" switch; the install and
      launch report does not, so it is sent even if you turn analytics off. We
      do not use this to advertise to you ourselves.
    </p>

    <h3>The tracking question on iPhone, and the advertising ID</h3>
    <p>
      On iPhone the app asks for permission under Apple's App Tracking
      Transparency. If you say yes, AppsFlyer can use your device's advertising
      identifier to match an install to an ad. If you say no, no advertising
      identifier is read, and AppsFlyer still receives the install and in-app
      events described above using its own identifiers. On Android the app
      declares the Google advertising ID permission, used for the same purpose;
      you can reset or delete your advertising ID in Android settings.
    </p>
    <p>
      Legal basis for install attribution: legitimate interest (Art. 6(1)(f)),
      and on iPhone your answer to the tracking question.
    </p>
    <p>
      We do not sell your data, and we do not show advertising inside the app.
    </p>

    <h2>4. How we use AI</h2>
    <p>
      WeHale uses an AI service from Anthropic, a company in the United States,
      in two places.
    </p>

    <h3>Session recommendations</h3>
    <p>
      When you open Home, and when you tap a mood, the app asks our server for
      session suggestions. The server sends the AI service: the mood you picked;
      the time of day; your experience level, goals and needs from your answers;
      the number of sessions in the last two weeks; up to ten recent sessions
      (title, category, date, your rating); up to ten check-in ratings from the
      last 14 days (before or after, the rating, the date); and our list of
      sessions. We do <strong>not</strong> send your name, your email address or
      your user ID. The service returns a short list of sessions and a one-line
      reason.
    </p>
    <p>
      Home asks for suggestions for all nine moods in advance, a few seconds
      after Home opens, so a tap is instant. That means your recent check-in
      ratings are sent without you tapping anything. If the AI service does not
      answer, the server picks sessions by simple rules instead, and nothing is
      sent. The suggestions are only suggestions: you can open any session you
      have access to. They do not have legal or similarly significant effects on
      you.
    </p>

    <h3>Support replies</h3>
    <p>
      When you send us a support message, our server sends the AI service your
      name, your email address, the date you joined, how many sessions you have
      finished, your membership status, the ticket category, your app version,
      your device type, and the conversation, so it can write a draft answer. A
      person on our team reads, edits and sends every reply. The AI never
      answers you by itself.
    </p>
    <p>
      The AI provider processes this data for us as our service provider. Legal
      basis: contract for the features you use (Art. 6(1)(b)).
    </p>

    <h2>5. Who receives your data</h2>
    <p>
      These companies process data for us, under contracts that limit them to
      our instructions.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Supabase</strong>: our database and file storage (feedback
        screenshots). Everything we store about your account. Hosted in
        Stockholm, Sweden.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Railway</strong>: hosts our server. Everything that passes
        through the server while you use the app.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Anthropic</strong>: AI recommendations and support drafts. What
        section 4 lists.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>AppsFlyer</strong>: install attribution and campaign
        measurement. Install and open events, device identifiers (including the
        advertising ID if you allow it on iPhone, and on Android), your user ID,
        the in-app events in section 3, and your IP address.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>RevenueCat</strong>: subscriptions. Your user ID and your
        subscription and purchase information.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Loops</strong>: email, such as newsletters and one-off emails you
        ask for. Your email address, first name, user ID, number of sessions
        finished, goal, experience level, and whether you agreed to marketing
        email.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Resend</strong>: account emails, for example the password reset
        code. Your email address and the message.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sentry</strong>: crash and performance reports. Crash details,
        device model, operating system and app version, and your user ID on the
        server side. The app does not send your name or email. Reports from the
        app go to Sentry's endpoint in Germany.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Expo, Apple (APNs) and Google (FCM)</strong>: delivering push
        notifications. Your push token and the notification text.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Apple and Google</strong>: Sign in with Apple and Sign in with
        Google, and your purchases through the stores. What they collect under
        their own policies.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Slack</strong>: our internal alerts. A support message's sender
        name, subject and the start of the text.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Google BigQuery</strong>: our internal reports. A pseudonymised
        copy of usage data, with no email, no name and no free text, and a code
        instead of your user ID. Hosted in the EU.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      We do not sell personal data and we do not share it with others for their
      own marketing.
    </p>
    <p>
      We may also disclose data where the law requires, to protect our legal
      rights, to professional advisers under confidentiality, and in a sale of
      the company, in which case we will tell you before your data is covered by
      a different policy.
    </p>

    <h2>6. Transfers outside the EU/EEA</h2>
    <p>
      Some of the companies above are in the United States or elsewhere outside
      the EU/EEA. Where they are, we rely on an adequacy decision (including the
      EU-US Data Privacy Framework where the company is certified) or on the EU
      Commission's Standard Contractual Clauses, together with technical
      measures such as encryption in transit. You can ask us for information
      about the safeguards at
      <a href="mailto:support@wehale.io">support@wehale.io</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>7. How long we keep data, and deleting your account</h2>
    <p>
      While your account is open we keep what is described in section 2.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Delete your account yourself:</strong> More &rarr; Profile &amp;
      Settings &rarr; Danger zone &rarr; Delete account. You can also email
      <a href="mailto:support@wehale.io">support@wehale.io</a> from the address
      on your account. The steps are on our
      <a href="/delete-account">delete account page</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>What happens when an account is deleted.</strong> Your account,
      practice history, check-ins, answers, reminders, goals, likes, milestones,
      subscription record on our side, push tokens, support tickets and
      messages, feedback and scores, and any welcome period are erased from our
      live database in one step. Your name and email are removed from the usage
      events that remain, and those events are no longer linked to you. Your
      feedback screenshots are removed from storage, and your contact is removed
      from Loops.
    </p>
    <p><strong>What we keep after deletion, and why:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        A record that the deletion happened: a one-way hash of your email
        address, which sign-in method you used, the date, and counts of what was
        removed. It lets us show that we honoured your request, and it cannot be
        turned back into your email address. We keep it for as long as we need
        to be able to show that, and we review it from time to time. Legal
        basis: legal obligation and our legitimate interest in being able to
        show compliance (Art. 6(1)(c) and (f)).
      </li>
      <li>
        Purchase records at RevenueCat and in the store receipts, because they
        are financial records, for as long as accounting law requires. Deleting
        your account does <strong>not</strong> cancel a subscription: cancel it
        first in your Apple or Google subscription settings.
      </li>
      <li>
        If you hold lifetime access from before: the email address and the fact
        of that access, so the access is not lost or duplicated.
      </li>
      <li>
        Usage events from which your name, email and ID were removed. They no
        longer point to a person.
      </li>
      <li>
        Copies in our database provider's backups, until they expire in the
        provider's normal backup cycle. Nothing from a backup is used for
        anything other than restoring the service.
      </li>
      <li>
        Copies held by our service providers under their own retention rules
        (for example AppsFlyer, Sentry, the AI provider, Resend and Slack). We do
        not currently send them a deletion request when an account is deleted.
        You can ask us to contact a provider for you.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Other data we keep for as long as needed for the purpose, and no longer:
      crash reports and diagnostics for the period set by our crash-reporting
      provider; support messages while your account exists; and usage events
      while your account exists, and in de-identified form afterwards.
    </p>

    <h2>8. Email</h2>
    <p>
      We send account emails (for example a password reset) because you need
      them to use the service.
    </p>
    <p>
      We send newsletters and updates only if you agreed. On the email sign-up
      form the box is empty until you tick it. If you signed up with Apple or
      Google, we ask once after you sign up, and "Not now" means no. You can
      change your mind at any time in Profile &amp; Settings (Newsletter &amp;
      tips) or with the unsubscribe link in every email. Legal basis: your
      consent (Art. 6(1)(a)).
    </p>
    <p>
      We also send one email before your offer ends if you ask for it ("Email me
      instead"). That does not subscribe you to the newsletter.
    </p>

    <h2>9. Your rights</h2>
    <p>
      Under the GDPR you have the right to: know what we hold and get a copy
      (access); have mistakes corrected; have your data erased; restrict how we
      use it; receive the data you gave us in a structured, machine-readable
      format (portability); object to processing based on our legitimate
      interest; withdraw consent at any time; and not be subject to a decision
      based only on automated processing with legal or similarly significant
      effect.
    </p>
    <p>
      To use a right, email
      <a href="mailto:support@wehale.io">support@wehale.io</a> from your account
      address. We reply within one month, as the law requires, and normally
      sooner. There is no export button in the app: for a copy of your data,
      email us.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can always delete your account in the app (section 7), switch off
      usage analytics and notifications in Profile &amp; Settings, and withdraw
      marketing consent there.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you think we handle your data wrongly, you can complain to the Swedish
      supervisory authority:
    </p>
    <p>
      Integritetsskyddsmyndigheten (IMY)<br />
      Website: www.imy.se<br />
      Email: imy@imy.se
    </p>
    <p>
      You may also complain to the authority in your own EU/EEA country. We
      would be glad to hear from you first, at
      <a href="mailto:support@wehale.io">support@wehale.io</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>10. Children</h2>
    <p>
      WeHale is intended for people aged 16 and over. We do not knowingly
      collect data from anyone younger. If you believe a younger person has
      given us data, write to
      <a href="mailto:support@wehale.io">support@wehale.io</a> and we will
      delete it.
    </p>

    <h2>11. Our website and cookies</h2>
    <p>
      On wehale.io we use strictly necessary cookies. Only if you accept them in
      the cookie banner, we also use Google Analytics (which pages are used) and
      Meta and TikTok measurement (which ads bring people to the site, and
      whether a session starts). You can change your choice at any time with
      "Privacy choices" at the bottom of every page. Legal basis: consent
      (Art. 6(1)(a) and the ePrivacy rules).
    </p>

    <h2>12. Security</h2>
    <p>
      Data is encrypted in transit (TLS) and passwords are stored hashed. Only
      the people who need access to run the service have it. No system is
      perfectly secure, and we will tell you and the authority if a breach
      affects you, as the law requires.
    </p>

    <h2>13. Changes</h2>
    <p>
      We will update this page and its date when something changes. If a change
      is important (new kinds of data, new purposes, new recipients) we will
      tell you in the app or by email before it applies.
    </p>

    <h2>14. Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Andningsappen Varberg AB (WeHale)<br />
      Boråsgatan 12 B, 432 45 Varberg, Sweden<br />
      Organisation number: 559496-1491<br />
      Email: <a href="mailto:support@wehale.io">support@wehale.io</a>
    </p>
  </LegalLayout>
</BaseLayout>
